WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.213
<v Examiner>Exam case 2.

2
00:00:02.213 --> 00:00:04.823
<v Examiner>The aftereffects of lymph node removal.

3
00:00:05.273 --> 00:00:07.791
<v Examiner>Aleki and Vicky visit their friend Mary,

4
00:00:07.791 --> 00:00:11.189
<v Examiner>who had a right mastectomy for cancer a fortnight ago.

5
00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:14.650
<v Examiner>Mary mentions the cancer had spread to the lymph nodes,

6
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.671
<v Examiner>and some axillary lymph nodes were also removed.

7
00:00:17.671 --> 00:00:21.509
<v Examiner>She complains of swelling in her right arm since the surgery.

8
00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:26.355
<v Examiner>Her friend, who is good with mirimiri and massage, offers to massage her arm,

9
00:00:26.355 --> 00:00:28.621
<v Examiner>which helps bring the swelling down.

10
00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:30.873
Three questions, seven marks.

11
00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:35.535
It's the shortest case in the exam, so these are the easiest marks to bank.

12
00:00:35.985 --> 00:00:36.678
<v Examiner>Three marks.

13
00:00:36.678 --> 00:00:39.739
<v Examiner>What are the major functions of the lymphatic system?

14
00:00:40.189 --> 00:00:42.066
Three functions, a mark each.

15
00:00:42.066 --> 00:00:44.136
The hook: digest, defend, drain.

16
00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:45.270
Digestive.

17
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:50.261
Lacteals in the gut absorb dietary fat, and carry it away for processing.

18
00:00:50.711 --> 00:00:51.622
Immunological.

19
00:00:51.622 --> 00:00:52.924
The immune response.

20
00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:55.072
The thymus, where T cells mature.

21
00:00:55.072 --> 00:00:59.433
The bone marrow, where B cells mature, and B cells make antibodies.

22
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:04.901
And the lymph nodes, where lymphocytes and other immune cells meet and coordinate an

23
00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:05.943
immune response.

24
00:01:06.393 --> 00:01:07.281
Fluid balance.

25
00:01:07.281 --> 00:01:11.277
It drains away the excess tissue fluid, the interstitial fluid,

26
00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:15.907
monitors it for antigens on the way, and returns it to the venous system.

27
00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:17.968
<v Examiner>Two marks.

28
00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:22.181
<v Examiner>What is the role of the axillary lymph nodes in the spread of cancer?

29
00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:22.669
<v Examiner>Explain.

30
00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:25.613
The spread of cancer is called metastasis.

31
00:01:25.613 --> 00:01:30.542
If a cancerous cell breaks away from its tumour and gets into the lymphatic system,

32
00:01:30.542 --> 00:01:33.274
it can potentially reach any part of the body.

33
00:01:33.724 --> 00:01:36.933
The breast drains to the axillary nodes, in the armpit,

34
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:39.034
so they're checked for cancer cells.

35
00:01:39.034 --> 00:01:43.002
The first node in line, closest to the breast, is the sentinel node.

36
00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.686
Cancer cells there show the cancer has spread.

37
00:01:45.686 --> 00:01:50.471
No cancer cells there gives some confidence that it's still limited to the breast.

38
00:01:51.921 --> 00:01:52.540
<v Examiner>Two marks.

39
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.755
<v Examiner>Why did Mary develop swelling in her right arm after the mastectomy?

40
00:01:56.755 --> 00:02:01.094
<v Examiner>Think about fluid dynamics, and what keeps fluid in the tissue spaces.

41
00:02:01.544 --> 00:02:03.538
When the lymph nodes are removed,

42
00:02:03.538 --> 00:02:08.069
the fluid that normally drains from the arm through those nodes is blocked.

43
00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:11.694
Normally, tissue fluid drains through the lymphatic vessels,

44
00:02:11.694 --> 00:02:15.017
and back into the venous system at the subclavian vein.

45
00:02:15.017 --> 00:02:16.527
Now it has nowhere to go.

46
00:02:16.527 --> 00:02:20.213
The lymphatic vessels fill until they can't drain the tissue,

47
00:02:20.213 --> 00:02:22.811
and fluid stays in the interstitial spaces.

48
00:02:22.811 --> 00:02:24.563
That swelling is lymphoedema.

49
00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:29.178
Her formative test turns this case into drop-downs,

50
00:02:29.178 --> 00:02:31.972
and the keys are worth knowing word for word.

51
00:02:31.972 --> 00:02:36.131
The role of the axillary nodes in the spread of cancer: metastasis.

52
00:02:36.131 --> 00:02:40.227
No cancer cells in them shows the cancer is limited to the breast.

53
00:02:40.227 --> 00:02:45.255
And Mary's swelling is a sign that the lymph drainage is hampered by the surgery.

54
00:02:45.705 --> 00:02:46.534
<v Examiner>Once more.

55
00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:47.113
<v Examiner>Case 2.

56
00:02:49.563 --> 00:02:51.923
The lymphatic system has three jobs.

57
00:02:51.923 --> 00:02:54.479
Digestive: lacteals absorb dietary fat.

58
00:02:54.479 --> 00:02:58.609
Immunological: the thymus, the bone marrow and the lymph nodes.

59
00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:03.460
And fluid balance: draining excess tissue fluid back to the venous system.

60
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:09.097
Cancer spreading is metastasis, and cells in the lymph can reach any part of the body.

61
00:03:09.097 --> 00:03:14.996
The axillary nodes are checked, the sentinel node first: cancer there means it has spread;

62
00:03:14.996 --> 00:03:17.946
none means it's likely limited to the breast.

63
00:03:17.946 --> 00:03:22.403
Removing the nodes blocks the arm's drainage to the subclavian vein,

64
00:03:22.403 --> 00:03:25.156
so fluid stays in the tissue: lymphoedema.
